= Goodryke and DuFour

At the meeting between [+ royalist_leader +], Gorrick, Goodryke, Meissner, and DuFour’s son, everyone—except Oskar—backs the appointment of [+ royalist_chancellor +] as Chancellor. Meissner’s shift is especially significant; once wary, he now lends weight to the schemers’ influence. The President, who once vowed never to appoint [+ royalist_chancellor +], begins to waver.

The next morning, Goodryke presents his plan to DuFour. His call for Staal’s dismissal meets no resistance. But when he names [+ royalist_chancellor +] as the preferred successor—rather than himself, as DuFour would prefer—the old man recoils. He remains scornful and deeply distrustful of [+ royalist_chancellor +], deeming him fit, at best, to oversee the postal ministry.

- @nazi_2

@nazi_2
title: Meissner and Oskar are called in.
on-arrival: dufour_gallax_r += 1

The President summons his chief of staff Meissner and his son Oskar to join the discussion. Meissner backs Goodryke’s proposal that [+ royalist_chancellor +] be appointed Chancellor, with Goodryke serving as Vice Chancellor. He argues that this arrangement is the only viable path out of the political deadlock—and the best way to tame the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span>, pointing to the considerable number of cabinet posts [+ royalist_leader +] has agreed to hand over to conservatives.

By the meeting’s end, however, both DuFour and his son remain unconvinced. They continue to press for Goodryke’s own return to the chancellorship instead.